{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-161722",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "A type III region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,z)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(x,z)\\le y\\le {u}_{2}(x,z)\\}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type III region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14217845/assets/0603bed12c6af74e46544d8bebc52362aed95a9a5ad7418ea4faaf228f3b5241.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type III region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x,z)=-z,\\,{u}_{2}(x,z)={x}^{2}+z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{S}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{-z}^{{x}^{2}+z}f(x,y,z)\\,dy]dA.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
